{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"OSS COMPARISON OF REBAR'S JIRA DATA CENTER INTEGRATION — findings, remediation, and what was NOT\nverifiable. Commissioned to check that our integration follows a well-trodden path and that any\ndivergence is intentional.\n\n=== THE HEADLINE: ONE DEFECT THAT WRITES WRONG DATA ===\n\n[rebar:21fc-51d7-90ca-4a03] DC INDEX LAG MAKES recover_pending CREATE DUPLICATE ISSUES. Verified in\nsource, not taken on trust. binding_store.py:521-531, keyless-pending branch, searches JQL for the\nrebar-id label and UNBINDS when the search returns nothing — its docstring says 'unbind if not (the\ncreate never reached Jira)'. But keyless-pending is entered PRECISELY when we crashed during\ncreate_issue, and DC's Lucene index is explicitly eventually consistent (Atlassian KB; JRASERVER-70423\ndocuments an index 2,991 seconds — ~50 min — behind). A JQL search cannot see an unindexed issue, so\non a lagging cluster recovery concludes the create never landed, unbinds, and the NEXT PASS CREATES A\nDUPLICATE.\nThe authors already understood this hazard: the KEYED branch's docstring says 'NO Jira search —\ndeterministic, so a hard crash in the create->label window yields NO duplicate.' They engineered it\naway where a key was known. The keyless branch had to search, and search is the index-dependent\noperation. Sound on Cloud, unsound on DC.\nThis is the ONLY finding in the whole review where rebar writes wrong data rather than reading\nincomplete data. Ranked must-fix.\n\n=== FIXED IMMEDIATELY, IN THE CHANGE THAT WAS STILL IN REVIEW ===\n\nPAGINATION TRUNCATION in get_parent_map — a data-loss bug in code I had pushed twenty minutes earlier.\nDC silently truncates maxResults above jira.search.views.default.max (Atlassian's REST reference and\nmaxResults KB both say so; a common hardening). My loop advanced by the REQUESTED page size and broke\non a short page, so a truncated FIRST page read as 'that is all there is'. Measured with a client\ncapping pages at 20: 20 of 250 parents recovered — 92% silently lost, raising nothing, and the inbound\npass would treat every unseen issue as parentless.\nFixed in change 1105 by advancing by len(batch) and breaking only on an EMPTY page, with a held-out\nregression test. Amended rather than filed as follow-up because the change had not landed.\n\n=== FILED FOR FOLLOW-UP ===\n\n[rebar:b586-2fda-8a15-4260] Rate limiting. DC-only, 8.6+, and OFF BY DEFAULT (admin-enabled), a\nper-user token bucket. So a static pacing policy is NOT implementable — the whole fix is to honour\nretry-after when present and degrade when absent. Atlassian prescribes retry-after PLUS up to 20%\nrandom jitter, which differs from rebar's ADR-0036 backoff curve.\n\n[rebar:387d-09c5-3d50-4a1e] A failed identity write DELETES the created issue. The mature answer is\nNOT to solve field discovery: Sentry's Jira integration explicitly declares it does not support custom\nrequired fields ('The only required fields supported are those that are pre-populated by Sentry').\nRemediation is (1) stop deleting — a created-but-unlabelled issue is recoverable via retro-attach, a\ndeleted one is not; (2) document the labels-on-screen requirement; (3) defer createmeta, possibly\nforever. Note the Jira error 'not on the appropriate screen' is misleading roughly half the time — it\nalso fires from the workflow property jira.permission.createclone.denied and from a MALFORMED value on\na field that IS on the screen.\n\n[rebar:7c26-4ac8-04a3-440e] Bindings key on the Jira KEY, which changes on project move (the numeric id\nnever does). DC-specific KB: third-party apps moving issues via post-functions/automations can fail to\nupdate the moved_issue_key table, after which the old key stops resolving entirely.\n\n=== EPISTEMIC NOTE — the research corrected ITSELF, and I am recording what stayed unverified ===\n\nThe agent's second pass RETRACTED two claims its first pass had stated firmly. I am recording that\nrather than quietly adopting the stronger version:\n * What GET /rest/api/2/issue/{oldKey} returns for a moved issue (200-with-new-key vs 301 vs 404) is\n   NOT established. The remediation (key on id) stands regardless; only the DETECTION mechanism is\n   unverified, and 7c26 carries an operator-attested AC to settle it on the harness.\n * The 'max.limit < default.max causes 403' claim is documented for search VIEWS, not for\n   /rest/api/2/search. TRUNCATION by default.max IS well-supported. The pagination fix is correct\n   under either reading, which is why it was safe to land.\nStill unresolved across both passes: PAT default expiry (one pass says 365-day max with a 10-token\ncap, the other says expiry is optional and defaults to none — likely version-dependent); whether\nPATs authenticate attachment retrieval (community-sourced only; rebar handles no attachments today);\nwhether 'total' on DC v2 is clamped (which is why the fix drives the loop off the returned page, not\noff total).\n\n=== CONVERGENCE: where we match the community, and where we do not ===\n\nThe comparison did not overturn the epic's core design decisions. pycontribs/jira as the DC client,\nPAT bearer auth, and an ephemeral Docker harness as the verification oracle are all consistent with\nwhat mature projects do — and the rejected PR that hand-rolled a REST v2 client would have diverged\nfrom that consensus, not toward it.\nWhat this review DID find is that our divergences are mostly not in the architecture but in the\nOPERATIONAL EDGES that only show up against a real, differently-configured instance: index lag,\nserver-side pagination caps, admin-toggled rate limiting, screen configuration, project moves. That is\na coherent pattern and worth stating plainly in J8's ADR: the seam design held up; the gaps were all\nin assuming a Cloud-shaped instance behaves like a DC-shaped one.\n\n=== WHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT THE METHOD ===\n\nEvery one of these was invisible to 1693 passing unit tests, a certified plan, and two green plan\nreviews. Two were invisible to the live harness as well, because the harness is a single\ndefault-configured 8.17.1 instance — it does not have a lagging index, a lowered\njira.search.views.default.max, rate limiting enabled, or a restricted create screen. So 'live testing\nis the oracle' is necessary and still not sufficient: the oracle only covers the configurations the\noracle happens to have."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785497236741833001,"uuid":"7126d8f9-3894-4518-8a17-449d9abaf1ce"}